+===============================================================================
+1. THE CHEAP DECISIVE PROBE — the design effect, never measured before
+===============================================================================
+L5 says per-decision scoring "buys roughly the ratio of decisions to games in
+effective sample".  That is true only if decisions are independent.  48
+independent 200-game blocks (seeds 74000001+), block-to-block SD against the
+binomial baseline:
+
+  arm           metric      p         dec/game   DEFF   eff.dec/game
+  v06           askAcc      0.54080     42.91    3.41      12.60
+  v06           declAcc     0.97806      4.49    1.03       4.35
+  v06           allocErr    0.01939      4.49    1.01       4.44
+  v07:r12=25    askAcc      0.53180     44.50    1.42      31.44
+  v07:r12=25    declAcc     0.96467      4.63    1.32       3.51
+  v07:r12=25    allocErr    0.02133      4.63    0.73       6.36
+
+Converted into one currency (games needed to resolve a 1.0-win-rate-point-
+equivalent effect at 2 sigma, using the ledger's own §0.2 conversion):
+
+  win rate                     9,604 games
+  declaration accuracy      284 – 560 games      (17x – 34x)
+  allocation-error share    244 – 299 games      (32x – 39x)
+
+=> L5's PRECISION arithmetic is CONFIRMED, with the clustering correction it
+   never had.  Deal-level clustering eats almost none of it on the declaration
+   channel (DEFF 1.0–1.3).  On the ask channel it eats two-thirds for v06
+   (DEFF 3.41) — and the corpus's "85 asks a game" counts both teams; one team
+   asks ~43 times a game.
